Tema 2 - BitTorrent Protocol

This is a simulation of the BitTorrent protocol, using OpenMPI to implement communication between a tracker and multiple clients.

Tracker:
    - Init phase: generally, the tracker only owns filenames and the hashes of their segments, as well as information regarding every file's swarm. In this phase, tracker awaits info about all existent files; when a message is received, tracker begins communication with that certain client,stopping only after all files from this source have been received. For each file, if it doesn't own it, the tracker adds it to 'swarms[]' with all associated data, else it just adds the client that sent info to the swarm, more specifically the seed swarm. In this phase there are no peers. When all clients have finished sending their owned files, tracker alerts them that they can start the download.
    - Download phase: here, the tracker waits for special messages that trigger different events:
        a. swarmlist: Since a client must have/update the swarm for the currently wanted file, the tracker is responsible of retrieving this data for it from 'swarms[]' by sending all associated data, especially seeds and peers. From this message, the tracker also learns to mark the process as peer.
        b. complete ___: This message alerts the tracker for a swarm update; now the current process is not a peer anymore, but a seed, so it owns all required segments.
        c. done: Helps keep track of active/inactive clients, so that tracker knows when to finish execution. (Download stops automatically for every client after they are done, but upload stays up until tracker shuts it down)

Client: Process begins execution by initializing its own info, then sending it to the tracker. After it receives the signal that it's okay to start, the implementation becomes multithreaded for faster communication, splitting up the download and upload tasks. The 2 threads use a shared structure to keep each other up to date with current file status. They must know at all times which files are owned ('owned_files') and which file is currently being downloaded ('crt_download' = filename, 'progress' = segments).
    - download_thread(): for every wanted file, client requests its swarm, marks it as current download and begins looking for every chunk. For optimization, client cycles between seeds and peers, collecting one chunk from each one of them until it has to start over. This way, clients don't leech off a single other process, making the others starve. The cycle looks like: seed[0] -> seed[1] -> ... -> seed [n] -> peer[0] -> peer[1] -> ... -> peer[n]. The swarm is periodically updated, every 10 segment downloads. After one complete download, tracker is notified and data is saved in a file. After the whole download process is done, tracker is notified again and the thread stops.
    - upload_thread(): upload thread can receive 2 types of messages:
        1. Tracker notification that app is closing up, and client needs to exit.
        2. Segment request from another client. Here, the process checks owned files, trying to match the requested name. If it's successful, it replies with an ACK (equivalent of an actual segment transfer). Else, it looks at the file that is currently being downloaded, checks the name and sees if it owns that chunk. Based on this, it can reply with ACK or BAD. For faster communication, I also did not add mutexes. In case the download thread JUST happened to download the segment but the upload replied with BAD, the downloader client can just move on to the next process that most probably will give that segment to him. Mutexes would just add overhead to an application whose reliability doesn't need synchronization.
    